WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.250
Let’s return to 2003, a time before YouTube.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.749
Yes, we know that’s a frightening thought,
we only ask that you take a deep breath and

00:00:08.749 --> 00:00:12.080
compose yourself so we can get through the
rest of the story.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.240
While YouTube may not have been invented for
another two years, the burgeoning internet

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:21.940
of the early 2000s still wasn’t without
its content creators, posting everywhere from

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:27.460
basic social media sites, to personal GeoCities
websites and specialty forums.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:32.210
One of these early proto-content-creators
was a man named John Cutler, better known

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:34.780
at the time by his online handle: Johnstation.

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:40.129
Johnstation was, tragically, a man operating
before his time.

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:45.920
His interests were firmly based on three things:
Mysteries, the paranormal and urban exploration.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:51.030
If he’d been operating a mere decade later,
he would have made a killing on Urbex videos,

00:00:51.030 --> 00:00:56.510
or Scary 3 AM Challenges, but instead, he
was just sharing his urban exploration photos

00:00:56.510 --> 00:01:02.230
and videos with a small group of friends on
various niche internet forums - like Parawatch,

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:06.930
a premiere destination for lovers of the strange
and unusual online.

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:11.950
Little did he know, an anomalous being he
was about to encounter was an even more prolific

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.950
internet user than him.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.520
But let’s start from the beginning.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.350
Johnstation was making his way to the city
of Detroit, Michigan, on a Greyhound bus.

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:26.200
Detroit had become somewhat of a holy land
for dark tourists, photographers, and urban

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:31.010
explorers - It’d once been one of the most
valuable manufacturing hubs in the United

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:36.270
States, the very heart of the automotive industry
until all of those manufacturing jobs were

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:39.119
outsourced to other countries for cheaper
labor.

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:43.930
This, along with a number of other factors,
led to a devastating economic collapse that

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:49.370
the area is still suffering from today, along
with a huge number of abandoned residential,

00:01:49.370 --> 00:01:54.200
commercial, and industrial buildings, just
waiting to be explored.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.680
And Johnstation planned to start off with
a real staple of the Urbex genre: A derelict

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:00.690
warehouse.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.450
He was expecting to get some cool photos of
disused machinery and maybe some cool graffiti

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:10.310
left by anonymous street artists over the
years, but what he certainly didn’t expect

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:15.950
was a series of thirteen massive underground
structures hidden below the warehouse.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.940
Something that looked like it would take decades
to even excavate the room for, let alone actually

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:21.980
build.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:27.750
The whole thing seemed like some kind of obscenely
massive workshop: Strange machinery, shelves

00:02:27.750 --> 00:02:32.680
upon shelves of papers and notes, spare parts
laying around everywhere.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.080
What had happened here?

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.830
But as Johnstation continued his search into
the bowels of this labyrinthine warehouse,

00:02:38.830 --> 00:02:44.510
he came upon the most strange and disquieting
discovery of all: A large glass jar filled

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.790
with green liquid, and a perfectly preserved
human brain floating inside.

00:02:49.790 --> 00:02:53.400
Of course, he took some pictures to share
with his forumite buddies and got the hell

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:54.900
out of dodge.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:01.230
Johnstation had made what was - for any self-respecting
Parawatch-user - a terrible mistake.

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:05.930
Because he was too busy listening to Avril
Lavigne on his big, clunky, early-2000s headphones,

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:09.130
he didn’t hear the brain’s attempt to
communicate with him.

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:12.700
“Oh, hello, young man, are you here to see
my marvelous inventions?

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:13.700
Young man?

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:14.700
Young man?”

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:16.040
But he was already gone by then.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:21.990
Little did Johnstation know, he’d just encountered
one of the greatest human minds of his generation:

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:25.220
Jeremy Valdez, an inventor without equal.

00:03:25.220 --> 00:03:31.350
And what’s more, he may have actually encountered
this singularly intelligent individual before.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:34.870
Thanks to Jeremy’s brilliant foresight,
and the invention of a little something he

00:03:34.870 --> 00:03:41.230
called the V-Wave Universal Ansible, this
disembodied brain was connected to the internet.

00:03:41.230 --> 00:03:44.100
What exactly was it doing with that internet
connection?

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:48.180
Everything from being a frequent forum poster
to selling anomalous technology on the deep

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:49.180
web.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:53.010
While we don’t know much more than that
about the brain’s browsing habits, it goes

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:57.010
without saying that we’d kill to see a copy
of its internet history.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:01.060
When Johnstation returned home, he posted
a picture of the brain to the internet, telling

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:04.410
his fellow urbex buddies to go and check the
warehouse out.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:08.760
Naturally, this caught the attention of the
SCP Foundation, who saw a mysterious human

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:15.130
brain hiding in a secretive underground structure
in Detroit to be a certifiably anomalous development.

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:18.790
And really, they didn’t know the half of
what they were about to get into.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:22.430
Welcome to the mind of Jeremy Valdez.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.500
After all the proper witnesses had been given
amnestic treatment, the Foundation secured

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:30.470
the warehouse and began exploratory missions
into its bowels.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:35.540
It was exactly as Johnstation reported: A
chaos of junk and shelves upon shelves of

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.370
almost incomprehensible notes and diagrams.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:42.251
While agents searched for the brain of Jeremy
Valdez, they encountered what would later

00:04:42.251 --> 00:04:48.880
be known as SCP-2099-B: His wondrous anomalous
creations.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.810
Most of these creations are robotic in nature,
with some of them being known as “Peripherals”

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:58.900
- creations made specifically to help Valdez
continue his research in his disembodied brain

00:04:58.900 --> 00:04:59.900
form.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:03.840
And they weren’t exactly pleased about the
Foundation intruding on Jeremy’s extremely

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:05.130
important research.

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:09.910
They tried to defend his secret lair beneath
the warehouse and were destroyed in the firefight

00:05:09.910 --> 00:05:11.990
with Foundation personnel.

00:05:11.990 --> 00:05:16.570
Only five peripherals are active now, which
the Foundation uses as collateral in their

00:05:16.570 --> 00:05:19.750
deal with Valdez for his continued cooperation.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:24.270
To put it less politely: If he doesn’t play
ball, then the last of his peripheries get

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.490
terminated.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:30.960
Thankfully for him and the Foundation operatives
assigned to his case, Jeremy is a highly cooperative

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:35.680
anomaly who has consistently behaved pleasantly
towards Foundation staff.

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:40.930
That’s right: He’s able to communicate
directly with others by anomalous means.

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:45.140
However, while having a direct line to the
mind of this enigmatic ball of gray matter

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:50.450
would seem like a dream to your typical researcher,
the cooperation of Jeremy Valdez has been

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:54.490
less valuable than the Foundation initially
believed it would be.

00:05:54.490 --> 00:06:00.040
This is largely because Jeremy’s mind appeared
to be pretty similar to his workspace: Strange,

00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:01.650
chaotic, and hard to decipher.

00:06:01.650 --> 00:06:05.790
When they first asked who exactly he was,
his response was:

00:06:05.790 --> 00:06:11.450
"I am the Profound Professor V, the genius
who invented the Quantum Pistol, the Rocket

00:06:11.450 --> 00:06:14.460
to Sagittarius, the Window to Other Worlds.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:16.760
My name is Jeremy Valdez.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:22.600
Jeremy, there was another Jeremy I met once,
well, lots, but the one stuck in my mind.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.010
Clever man, a salesman.

00:06:24.010 --> 00:06:28.030
Can't trust them an inch if you give them
a mile, but that wasn't me."

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:30.580
He’s a little scatterbrained, to put it
politely.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:34.830
Many of his answers, while strange and amusing,
have been largely useless to the Foundation.

00:06:34.830 --> 00:06:39.310
For example, when they asked him how he was
able to construct the secret facility, he

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:40.310
said:

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:41.310
"Oh, dug it all out.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:42.510
Tricky bit was the supports.

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:44.520
Used solid Valdezium to hold it up.

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.520
That's my own invention.

00:06:45.520 --> 00:06:49.180
Name trademarked, patent pending, unless I
forgot to send the application."

00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:53.590
And when they asked how he was able to survive
as a mere brain in a jar, he had the equally

00:06:53.590 --> 00:06:55.530
baffling and frustrating response:

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:57.080
"Good diet and exercise.

00:06:57.080 --> 00:06:58.080
Healthy living.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.080
Oh, and the electrolytes.

00:06:59.080 --> 00:07:01.660
It's in my notes somewhere, the whole process.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:04.560
Never will look at mashed potatoes the same
way, I'll tell you that."

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:09.590
There may at least be some truth to that last
one, as the solution inside Jeremy’s jar

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:14.570
has been tested and proven to be water mixed
with electrolytes, sugars, green food coloring,

00:07:14.570 --> 00:07:16.210
and artificial flavoring.

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.720
The current working theory on the green food
coloring is simply that Jeremy thought it

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:22.660
would make his disembodied brain look cooler.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:27.840
Jeremy truly proves that the lines between
genius, madness, and outright buffoonery are

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.510
far more malleable than any of us could have
imagined.

00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:35.150
It’s because of Jeremy’s forgetfulness
that he and his peripheries are such meticulous

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:40.420
note-takers, though there is no discernable
filing system for this absurd multitude of

00:07:40.420 --> 00:07:44.540
notes, and the majority of them are written
in a quick and scratchy shorthand that only

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:47.440
Jeremy himself seems able to decipher.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:51.980
But of course, the most fascinating part of
the story of Jeremy Valdez is his creations,

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:56.340
so now, we’re going to take a better look
at those inventions, and some of the stress

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.460
that the Foundation has been going through
in order to track the rest of them down.

00:08:00.460 --> 00:08:03.030
The peripherals are a varied bunch.

00:08:03.030 --> 00:08:07.199
Some are as simple as a pair of robot hands
that endlessly take notes from Jeremy’s

00:08:07.199 --> 00:08:09.650
unstoppable stream of consciousness.

00:08:09.650 --> 00:08:14.080
Some are as theatrical as humanoid robots
dressed like butlers - we can’t help but

00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:18.980
wonder how Mr. Deeds from SCP-662 would feel
about the extra competition.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:23.280
However, the peripherals are really just the
tip of the iceberg when it comes to Jeremy’s

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:24.570
overall body of work.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:28.380
Let’s take a little crash course through
some of the peculiar inventions they discovered

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.380
in Detroit.

00:08:29.380 --> 00:08:35.550
A pistol that fires out powerful x-rays, inexplicably
powered by two ordinary AA batteries and focused

00:08:35.550 --> 00:08:37.960
via a non-anomalous quartz crystal.

00:08:37.960 --> 00:08:42.390
A six-meter-tall humanoid robot constructed
primarily from chrome and steel.

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.120
A cannon that fires anomalous robot ninjas.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:47.230
No, that isn’t a joke.

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:51.800
The cannon fires cartridges that anomalously
break open, revealing human-sized robots wielding

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:53.010
swords and nunchucks.

00:08:53.010 --> 00:08:57.980
A bin labeled “cyborg parts”, which was
filled with a multitude of cybernetic prosthetic

00:08:57.980 --> 00:09:02.310
body parts - though none of these parts had
any mechanisms that would allow them to interface

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.320
with the human nervous system.

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:06.279
But wait, there’s more!

00:09:06.279 --> 00:09:11.130
A huge excavation vehicle with massive drills
and claws that makes the dirt it displaces

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:15.640
disappear, perhaps explaining how Jeremy managed
this little construction project.

00:09:15.640 --> 00:09:20.140
A rocket-based spacecraft with sediment stuck
to its landing gear that indicates it’s

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:22.520
been on the moon at some point in the past.

00:09:22.520 --> 00:09:26.900
A forcefield generator so effective that the
Foundation is still unable to penetrate it

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.960
because Jeremy himself has - unsurprisingly
- forgotten how to turn it off.

00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:36.890
A failed anti-gravity machine that instead
just creates more gravity, meaning anything

00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:40.831
or anyone brought into its vicinity becomes
twice as heavy as they would be under the

00:09:40.831 --> 00:09:43.510
normal conditions of Earth gravity.

00:09:43.510 --> 00:09:47.930
In case you needed a little dose of existential
terror today, there’s also a complete working

00:09:47.930 --> 00:09:54.360
simulation of an entire alien solar system,
somehow held on only two gigabytes of memory.

00:09:54.360 --> 00:09:59.200
And of course, we wouldn’t be doing Jeremy
Valdez justice if we didn’t tell you how

00:09:59.200 --> 00:10:01.700
obsessed he is with lasers.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:06.860
The Foundation even found a large containment
unit on the site, labeled “713 different

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:13.670
laser guns”, which contained - Yep, you
guessed it - 713 different laser guns.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:17.640
We imagine at least one or two of these ended
up in the Foundation armory, or at least the

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.440
personal office of Dr. Bright - Well, if they
could only figure out how this absurd, ridiculous

00:10:22.440 --> 00:10:24.620
technology actually worked.

00:10:24.620 --> 00:10:29.730
The most challenging part of containing SCP-2099
isn’t the brain itself - though the picture

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:33.750
of Jeremy’s brain is still circulating out
on the internet today if you know where to

00:10:33.750 --> 00:10:38.510
look - it’s finding and containing the anomalous
items that Jeremy left laying around in his

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.750
life before containment.

00:10:40.750 --> 00:10:45.350
Because Jeremy himself has a brain like a
colander, even finding these items is largely

00:10:45.350 --> 00:10:51.230
a result of picking up clues from his bizarre
ramblings, and the thousands of pages of nigh-incomprehensible

00:10:51.230 --> 00:10:53.680
notes he won’t stop babbling on about.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:58.690
While trying to find one of Jeremy’s items
in an alley in Boston, Massachusetts, Foundation

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:03.290
agents ran afoul of a group of Chaos Insurgency
agents searching for the same thing.

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:07.980
But this wouldn’t be the last peculiar tie
between Jeremy and certain groups of interest

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:09.880
pitted against the Foundation.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:14.220
In a soup kitchen in Seattle, Washington,
the Foundation found that Jeremy had once

00:11:14.220 --> 00:11:19.810
sold an enlarger ray to agents of the Manna
Charitable Foundation - A surprisingly benevolent

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.600
GOI who use anomalies to help the needy.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:27.480
They used the enlarger ray to enlarge food
and fight against hunger in the city’s homeless

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:28.480
population.

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:33.360
And while visiting an area in Boise, Idaho,
after Jeremy once mentioned having customers

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:38.060
there, the Foundation discovered substantial
connections to a member of a Serpent’s Hand

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:39.060
cell.

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:43.480
It seemed to all observers that Jeremy was
a kind of anomalous Forrest Gump - a man who

00:11:43.480 --> 00:11:47.630
had somehow bungled his way through a series
of unfortunate arrangements with several of

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:49.630
the Foundation’s greatest enemies.

00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:53.050
He’s a brain with an awful lot of bad luck.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:54.730
Or is he?

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:59.040
Because while the Foundation has bought his
bumbling fool schtick hook, line, and sinker,

00:11:59.040 --> 00:12:01.460
that doesn’t mean it’s true.

00:12:01.460 --> 00:12:05.180
In fact, in a classified letter to a member
of the Serpent’s Hand that worked closely

00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:08.940
with him in the past, Jeremy presented a very
different picture of himself…

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:10.770
“Hey, L, I got your letter.

00:12:10.770 --> 00:12:13.270
Yeah, I'm not too worried about the SeCoPro.

00:12:13.270 --> 00:12:15.589
They're still digging at the old place.

00:12:15.589 --> 00:12:17.250
Psychology of the individual, man.

00:12:17.250 --> 00:12:18.250
They're like cats.

00:12:18.250 --> 00:12:20.230
You run, they're gonna chase you.

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:24.730
But if they see something else moving, that's
where their eyes are, leaving you to jet.

00:12:24.730 --> 00:12:25.730
The tail?

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:26.740
Yeah, he's fine.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.610
He's designed for this stuff, man.

00:12:28.610 --> 00:12:30.480
And it's not like he's really there or anything.

00:12:30.480 --> 00:12:33.360
They get wise, we pull the plug and get away
free and clear.

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:36.170
Anyway, I got some new toys I came up with.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:37.170
Robots the size of fleas.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:39.240
They grab onto a guy, get carried wherever.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:43.899
They record what they see and hear, leave
with whoever, and detach where you need 'em.

00:12:43.899 --> 00:12:46.050
No signal to trace, because they don't broadcast.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.980
You get it all when you collect them.

00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:50.890
Figure it'll be useful for that project of
yours.

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:54.330
I know, not your usual style, but get with
the times, man.

00:12:54.330 --> 00:12:58.500
If you'd care to join me in the 22nd century,
leave payment in the usual place.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.779
Ask Schitz if you don't remember.

00:13:00.779 --> 00:13:03.690
And please, man, let me take a look at that
hat.

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:05.660
I just know I can make it better.

00:13:05.660 --> 00:13:08.360
You could have goddamned lasers firing off
your head.

00:13:08.360 --> 00:13:09.360
Think about it.

00:13:09.360 --> 00:13:10.360
V.”

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:11.360
So, what do you think?

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:15.700
Is Jeremy a bumbling brain in a jar, or is
that very brain a decoy for one of the most

00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:21.440
ingenious and deceptive masterminds in the
anomalous world, an anomalous Keyser Soze,

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:26.110
always staying one step ahead of the Foundation
while sending its agents on wild goose chases?

00:13:26.110 --> 00:13:32.410
Well, the fact we even need to ask shows that
Jeremy Valdez is a few steps ahead of us already...

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:38.750
Now go check out “SCP-5049 - The Skin Salesman
- Demon Dan's Discount Homunculus Depot”

00:13:38.750 --> 00:13:44.150
and “A Sea Snail's Fantasy SCP-1867 - A
Gentleman” for more SCPs you could hold

00:13:44.150 --> 00:13:46.170
a very interesting conversation with!

